1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,610
On the twenty-third of
March, twenty twenty-one,

2
00:00:02,900 --> 00:00:05,530
one ship turned sideways
in the Suez Canal.

3
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:08,650
And for six days, nothing
could get past it.

4
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:11,610
Behind it, hundreds of ships piled up.

5
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:15,270
Oil tankers, car carriers,
other giant container ships,

6
00:00:15,460 --> 00:00:17,290
and ships full of live animals.

7
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:20,590
Every single day, about
nine point six billion

8
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:23,490
dollars' worth of goods were
stuck in that traffic jam.

9
00:00:23,540 --> 00:00:25,110
And the first machine that showed up

10
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:28,090
to dig it out was one small excavator.

11
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:31,110
Next to a ship four hundred metres long.

12
00:00:31,220 --> 00:00:32,970
The whole internet laughed.

13
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:35,210
But behind the jokes was a real problem.

14
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:36,990
Nobody knew how to move a ship that

15
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:39,010
heavy out of a sandbank that tight.

16
00:00:39,260 --> 00:00:41,130
And some experts said it could take weeks.

17
00:00:41,420 --> 00:00:43,710
So how did one ship get stuck so badly?

18
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:45,930
How did they finally get it out?

19
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:49,130
And why was it then held prisoner
for more than three months?

20
00:00:49,420 --> 00:00:51,510
The ship is called Ever Given.

21
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:54,270
It was built in Japan by Imabari

22
00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:56,870
Shipbuilding and finished
in twenty eighteen.

23
00:00:57,280 --> 00:00:59,430
It's owned by a Japanese company,

24
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:02,590
run by a Taiwanese shipping
line called Evergreen,

25
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:06,150
managed by a German company,
and it flies the flag of Panama.

26
00:01:06,420 --> 00:01:07,750
That's normal in shipping.

27
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:09,890
One ship can belong to half the world.

28
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:11,240
Now the numbers.

29
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:13,610
It's almost exactly four
hundred metres long.

30
00:01:13,980 --> 00:01:16,090
That's nearly four
football pitches in a row.

31
00:01:16,260 --> 00:01:18,370
Stand it on its end, and it's taller than

32
00:01:18,420 --> 00:01:20,550
the roof of the Empire State Building.

33
00:01:20,780 --> 00:01:22,930
It's almost fifty-nine metres wide.

34
00:01:22,980 --> 00:01:26,710
And it can carry more than twenty
thousand standard containers.

35
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:29,710
If you took twenty thousand
of those containers

36
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:32,550
and put them in one line, end to end,

37
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:36,030
that line would be about a hundred
and twenty kilometres long.

38
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:38,870
That's more than half the
length of the Suez Canal.

39
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:42,350
Its engine is as powerful as about
seventy-nine thousand horses.

40
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:44,990
And at the bow there
are two side thrusters,

41
00:01:45,120 --> 00:01:47,370
small propellers that
push the front sideways.

42
00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:48,590
Remember those.

43
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:49,640
They won't be enough.

44
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:52,650
In March twenty twenty-one,
Ever Given is on its

45
00:01:52,700 --> 00:01:55,170
way from Malaysia to
Rotterdam, in the Netherlands.

46
00:01:55,940 --> 00:01:57,910
The fastest way from Asia to Europe goes

47
00:01:57,960 --> 00:01:59,770
through one narrow ditch in the desert.

48
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:02,530
The Suez Canal cuts through Egypt and

49
00:02:02,580 --> 00:02:04,510
joins the Red Sea to the Mediterranean.

50
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:06,590
It opened in eighteen sixty-nine.

51
00:02:07,060 --> 00:02:10,050
Without it, a ship from
Asia to Europe has to sail

52
00:02:10,100 --> 00:02:13,450
all the way around Africa,
past the Cape of Good Hope.

53
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:15,610
That can add up to two weeks to the trip.

54
00:02:15,660 --> 00:02:17,770
So a huge share of world
trade goes through here.

55
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:21,690
Estimates are around twelve percent
of everything traded by sea.

56
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:23,050
But here's the thing.

57
00:02:23,380 --> 00:02:27,150
The canal is about a hundred and
ninety-three kilometres long,

58
00:02:27,220 --> 00:02:29,950
and for big parts of
it, it's only one lane.

59
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:32,370
Ships can't pass each other there.

60
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:35,290
So they travel in convoys,
one behind the other,

61
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:37,170
like cars through roadworks.

62
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:39,690
One convoy goes north,
then one goes south.

63
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:42,430
Every ship takes Egyptian
canal pilots on board,

64
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:44,550
local experts who know the waterway.

65
00:02:44,840 --> 00:02:47,510
And there are speed limits,
because a big ship moving too

66
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:50,710
fast in shallow, narrow water
starts doing strange things.

67
00:02:51,240 --> 00:02:52,970
Hold on to that idea.

68
00:02:53,140 --> 00:02:55,030
Because that's exactly what happened.

69
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:58,990
It's early on Tuesday,
the twenty-third of March.

70
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:03,610
Ever Given enters the canal from
the south, near the city of Suez.

71
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:05,490
It's part of the northbound convoy.

72
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:06,670
The weather is bad.

73
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:08,970
A sandstorm is blowing across the desert.

74
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:12,910
Visibility is poor, and wind gusts
are reaching more than forty knots.

75
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:15,690
That's over seventy kilometres an hour.

76
00:03:15,860 --> 00:03:17,870
Now think about what that wind is hitting.

77
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:21,230
Ever Given is stacked high with
containers, from bow to stern.

78
00:03:21,540 --> 00:03:23,630
That's a steel wall four
hundred metres long.

79
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:27,410
In strong wind, a ship like
that is basically a giant sail.

80
00:03:27,860 --> 00:03:31,370
Just before seven forty in the
morning, the ship starts to drift.

81
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:34,290
The pilot orders the rudder
to port, to the left.

82
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:35,670
Then hard to port.

83
00:03:35,720 --> 00:03:36,970
The ship doesn't come back.

84
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:39,010
It's now close to the left bank,

85
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:41,670
and its speed has climbed
to around thirteen knots.

86
00:03:41,720 --> 00:03:44,610
The canal's speed limit
works out to under nine.

87
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:46,330
There's discussion on the bridge.

88
00:03:46,620 --> 00:03:49,150
The engine is slowed down,
then pushed back up to

89
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:51,950
full ahead to get more water
flowing over the rudder.

90
00:03:52,140 --> 00:03:53,250
Hard to port again.

91
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:55,150
But the bow keeps swinging to the right.

92
00:03:55,520 --> 00:03:56,520
Across the canal.

93
00:03:56,680 --> 00:03:57,890
Towards the other bank.

94
00:03:58,140 --> 00:04:00,350
At seven forty-one, Ever Given drives its

95
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:02,690
bow into the east bank
at about twelve knots.

96
00:04:03,020 --> 00:04:06,130
The stern swings round and
digs into the west bank.

97
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:08,090
Four hundred metres of ship,

98
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:10,550
in a canal that's narrower
than the ship is long.

99
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:12,810
It's wedged completely across.

100
00:04:14,060 --> 00:04:16,850
Kilometre one hundred and fifty-one
of the Suez Canal is closed.

101
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:19,130
The first pictures shocked everyone.

102
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:21,170
The bow wasn't just touching the bank.

103
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:23,050
It was buried deep in it.

104
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:24,810
And at the foot of that giant bow,

105
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:28,530
there was one small yellow
excavator, scooping away at the sand.

106
00:04:29,080 --> 00:04:30,990
That photo went around the world.

107
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:33,230
It became the joke of the year.

108
00:04:33,480 --> 00:04:36,110
Everyone suddenly felt like
the guy in that excavator,

109
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:38,070
facing a problem way too big for them.

110
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:40,390
But the real problem wasn't funny.

111
00:04:40,780 --> 00:04:43,910
Ever Given weighs around two hundred
thousand tonnes when it's loaded.

112
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:46,350
The front end was
sitting on sand and clay.

113
00:04:46,680 --> 00:04:48,650
The back end was jammed
into the other side.

114
00:04:48,900 --> 00:04:52,450
Pushing it in the wrong place could
crack the hull or bend the ship.

115
00:04:53,140 --> 00:04:56,130
The first tugs arrived the
same day and started pulling.

116
00:04:56,460 --> 00:04:57,830
The ship didn't move.

117
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:00,250
On the twenty-fifth of March,

118
00:05:00,420 --> 00:05:03,110
the canal authority officially
stopped all traffic.

119
00:05:03,660 --> 00:05:06,350
Now the line behind Ever
Given starts to grow.

120
00:05:06,660 --> 00:05:07,660
And grow.

121
00:05:07,720 --> 00:05:09,445
After a few days, at least three

122
00:05:09,495 --> 00:05:11,450
hundred and sixty-nine ships are waiting.

123
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:14,790
By the time the canal reopens,
it's more than four hundred.

124
00:05:15,640 --> 00:05:18,910
The shipping newspaper Lloyd's
List did a rough calculation.

125
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:21,850
Around five point one billion
dollars of goods a day were

126
00:05:21,900 --> 00:05:25,330
stuck going west, and four
point five billion going east.

127
00:05:25,540 --> 00:05:29,050
Together, about nine point six
billion dollars every single day.

128
00:05:29,100 --> 00:05:30,390
Some ships couldn't wait.

129
00:05:30,540 --> 00:05:33,590
They gave up and turned around,
taking the long way round Africa,

130
00:05:33,640 --> 00:05:36,830
adding days of sailing
and tonnes of extra fuel.

131
00:05:37,220 --> 00:05:40,150
And some of those waiting ships
were carrying live animals.

132
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:42,250
Sheep and cattle stuck at sea,

133
00:05:42,300 --> 00:05:44,890
with feed and water
running down every day.

134
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,210
Factories in Europe waited for parts.

135
00:05:47,580 --> 00:05:48,850
Shops waited for stock.

136
00:05:48,900 --> 00:05:50,810
And everyone was asking the same question.

137
00:05:51,180 --> 00:05:52,180
How long?

138
00:05:52,260 --> 00:05:55,430
The ship's owners hired
the experts: Smit Salvage,

139
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:57,310
part of the Dutch company Boskalis.

140
00:05:57,540 --> 00:05:59,150
Salvage teams like this are the people

141
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:01,430
you call when a ship
is in serious trouble.

142
00:06:01,660 --> 00:06:02,810
Their plan had three parts.

143
00:06:02,860 --> 00:06:03,860
Dig.

144
00:06:04,140 --> 00:06:04,910
Pull.

145
00:06:04,960 --> 00:06:06,810
And wait for the right tide.

146
00:06:07,420 --> 00:06:08,670
First, dig.

147
00:06:08,940 --> 00:06:10,470
The canal authority brought in its big

148
00:06:10,520 --> 00:06:12,830
cutter suction dredger, the Mashhour.

149
00:06:13,060 --> 00:06:16,370
It cuts into the sand under water
and sucks it away through a pipe,

150
00:06:16,680 --> 00:06:18,870
about two thousand cubic metres an hour.

151
00:06:19,100 --> 00:06:22,330
Smaller dredgers and excavators
worked closer to the ship.

152
00:06:22,580 --> 00:06:26,530
In total, about thirty thousand
cubic metres of sand were removed.

153
00:06:26,900 --> 00:06:29,510
That's enough to fill twelve
Olympic swimming pools.

154
00:06:29,860 --> 00:06:30,850
Second, pull.

155
00:06:30,900 --> 00:06:33,730
Eleven harbour tugs
worked around the ship.

156
00:06:33,880 --> 00:06:37,010
Then two powerful sea-going
tugs arrived to help:

157
00:06:37,180 --> 00:06:40,730
the Dutch Alp Guard and
the Italian Carlo Magno.

158
00:06:40,780 --> 00:06:43,170
Alp Guard alone can pull with a force of

159
00:06:43,220 --> 00:06:46,050
about two hundred and eighty-five tonnes.

160
00:06:46,400 --> 00:06:48,570
And there was a backup plan nobody wanted.

161
00:06:48,620 --> 00:06:50,330
If nothing worked, they'd have to lift

162
00:06:50,380 --> 00:06:52,410
off containers to make the ship lighter.

163
00:06:52,660 --> 00:06:55,830
But that meant bringing giant
cranes to a sandy canal bank,

164
00:06:55,880 --> 00:06:57,130
and it could have taken weeks.

165
00:06:57,640 --> 00:06:59,070
Third, the tide.

166
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:00,250
And here the Moon helped.

167
00:07:00,300 --> 00:07:04,750
At the end of March there was a full
moon close to Earth, a supermoon.

168
00:07:04,980 --> 00:07:07,850
That gives extra-high
tides, called spring tides.

169
00:07:08,060 --> 00:07:10,990
A little more water under the
hull can make a huge difference.

170
00:07:11,340 --> 00:07:13,950
Day after day, they
dug, pulled and pushed.

171
00:07:14,320 --> 00:07:15,550
The bow thrusters ran.

172
00:07:15,840 --> 00:07:17,790
The tugs pulled from different angles.

173
00:07:18,220 --> 00:07:21,170
Then, very early on Monday
the twenty-ninth of March,

174
00:07:21,240 --> 00:07:24,370
at about four thirty in the
morning, the stern came free.

175
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:26,590
The bow was still stuck.

176
00:07:26,740 --> 00:07:29,090
But the team kept going
as the tide came up again.

177
00:07:29,140 --> 00:07:31,710
And at five past three in the afternoon,

178
00:07:31,880 --> 00:07:33,870
Ever Given finally floated free.

179
00:07:34,180 --> 00:07:35,830
Tugboats blew their horns.

180
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:37,750
People on the banks cheered.

181
00:07:37,960 --> 00:07:40,890
After six days, the Suez
Canal was open again.

182
00:07:40,940 --> 00:07:42,890
Ships started moving that same evening.

183
00:07:43,100 --> 00:07:44,830
So was it just the wind?

184
00:07:45,060 --> 00:07:45,930
Not quite.

185
00:07:45,980 --> 00:07:47,670
There were a few things working together.

186
00:07:48,020 --> 00:07:49,190
First, the wind.

187
00:07:49,420 --> 00:07:52,370
A ship with that much side
area gets pushed hard.

188
00:07:52,620 --> 00:07:55,010
Second, something called the bank effect.

189
00:07:55,060 --> 00:07:57,690
When a big ship gets close to
one side of a narrow channel,

190
00:07:57,740 --> 00:08:01,390
water squeezes between the hull
and the bank and speeds up.

191
00:08:01,560 --> 00:08:03,090
Fast water means low pressure.

192
00:08:03,420 --> 00:08:05,590
So the stern gets sucked towards the bank,

193
00:08:05,640 --> 00:08:07,210
and the bow gets pushed away.

194
00:08:07,560 --> 00:08:10,030
The faster the ship goes,
the stronger it gets.

195
00:08:10,200 --> 00:08:11,470
That matches what happened.

196
00:08:11,800 --> 00:08:14,690
The ship was close to the
left bank, it was going fast,

197
00:08:14,740 --> 00:08:16,990
and the bow swung right across the canal.

198
00:08:17,580 --> 00:08:18,750
Third, the people.

199
00:08:18,980 --> 00:08:21,010
In twenty twenty-three, Panama,

200
00:08:21,120 --> 00:08:24,130
where the ship is registered,
published its investigation.

201
00:08:24,500 --> 00:08:26,630
It said the wind and poor visibility

202
00:08:26,680 --> 00:08:28,790
weren't properly treated as a risk.

203
00:08:28,980 --> 00:08:31,790
The ship went faster than the
canal's average speed limit.

204
00:08:32,040 --> 00:08:34,710
No tug escort was used,
even though the canal

205
00:08:34,760 --> 00:08:37,250
rules call for one in
conditions like that.

206
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:40,750
It also said the two pilots
talked to each other in Arabic,

207
00:08:40,940 --> 00:08:43,990
and the ship's officers couldn't
follow what was being decided.

208
00:08:44,180 --> 00:08:48,050
And it reminded everyone of
one rule: in the Suez Canal,

209
00:08:48,220 --> 00:08:50,010
the captain is always in command.

210
00:08:50,420 --> 00:08:51,710
The pilot only advises.

211
00:08:51,760 --> 00:08:54,250
The canal authority
and the ship's insurers

212
00:08:54,300 --> 00:08:56,670
argued for months about who was to blame.

213
00:08:56,860 --> 00:08:59,490
And that argument turned into
the next part of the story.

214
00:09:00,160 --> 00:09:01,410
The ship was free.

215
00:09:01,460 --> 00:09:02,950
But it wasn't going anywhere.

216
00:09:03,160 --> 00:09:06,710
It was taken to the Great Bitter
Lake, a wide part of the canal.

217
00:09:06,900 --> 00:09:10,430
And on the thirteenth of April, an
Egyptian court ordered it seized.

218
00:09:10,700 --> 00:09:12,330
The canal authority asked for more than

219
00:09:12,380 --> 00:09:14,760
nine hundred and sixteen million dollars.

220
00:09:14,920 --> 00:09:18,170
That was for the salvage
work, the lost canal fees,

221
00:09:18,320 --> 00:09:20,330
and damage to the canal's reputation.

222
00:09:20,720 --> 00:09:23,270
The owners said that was far too much.

223
00:09:23,420 --> 00:09:27,070
Later, the demand came down to
five hundred and fifty million.

224
00:09:27,460 --> 00:09:29,350
The negotiations went on for weeks.

225
00:09:29,440 --> 00:09:32,990
And while the lawyers talked, the
twenty-five crew stayed on board,

226
00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:35,130
stuck in the middle of
the desert with the ship.

227
00:09:35,380 --> 00:09:36,710
And so did all the cargo.

228
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:38,430
Thousands of containers of other

229
00:09:38,480 --> 00:09:40,850
people's goods that just couldn't leave.

230
00:09:41,720 --> 00:09:45,190
Finally, on the seventh of
July, a deal was signed.

231
00:09:45,520 --> 00:09:47,210
The exact amount was never made public.

232
00:09:47,260 --> 00:09:50,550
Part of the deal was a
new tugboat for the canal.

233
00:09:51,320 --> 00:09:52,870
Ever Given left Egypt,

234
00:09:53,040 --> 00:09:56,870
and on the twenty-ninth of July
it finally reached Rotterdam.

235
00:09:57,040 --> 00:09:59,490
More than four months after
it was meant to get there.

236
00:10:00,100 --> 00:10:03,270
After the blockage, Egypt sped
up work to fix that narrow

237
00:10:03,320 --> 00:10:06,670
southern part of the canal,
right where Ever Given got stuck.

238
00:10:07,060 --> 00:10:10,630
The canal was widened by about
forty metres along the east bank,

239
00:10:10,880 --> 00:10:13,350
and made deeper, over
about thirty kilometres.

240
00:10:13,740 --> 00:10:16,050
And a new ten-kilometre
second lane was dug,

241
00:10:16,100 --> 00:10:18,050
so more ships can pass each other.

242
00:10:18,660 --> 00:10:20,650
The two-way part of the canal grew from

243
00:10:20,700 --> 00:10:22,930
seventy-two to eighty-two kilometres.

244
00:10:23,540 --> 00:10:25,730
The new lane was tested in
late twenty twenty-four.

245
00:10:26,080 --> 00:10:27,250
And Ever Given?

246
00:10:27,620 --> 00:10:29,310
It was repaired, and by the end of

247
00:10:29,360 --> 00:10:31,810
twenty twenty-one, it was back at work.

248
00:10:32,140 --> 00:10:34,610
It's still sailing today,
still carrying twenty

249
00:10:34,660 --> 00:10:37,110
thousand containers
between Asia and Europe.

250
00:10:37,340 --> 00:10:38,430
Through the Suez Canal.

251
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:42,210
